window.JOHANSSON_RESIDENT_GUIDE = [{"name":"Johansson","role":"Chief engineer of the Minato Maru","place":"Minato Maru; home with Thuan on Kitano-jima","bio":"Johansson keeps the ferry’s machinery running, with a duty cabin aboard and his primary home with Thuan on Kitano-jima.","backstory":"Johansson is chief engineer of the Minato Maru. His notebooks hold machinery readings, maintenance jobs and the small details that make a reliable crossing. Nakandakari is his experienced relief engineer; Tetsuo maintains the electrical equipment under contract. He and Thuan share their primary home on Kitano-jima. He has a duty cabin aboard the ferry, while Thuan keeps her weekday room with Thao, Chin and Vy at 1 Kitahama. Neither arrangement creates another empty house in Minato. Lunch is often at Sato Ramen. A broken radio still brings him to Chin and Tetsuo’s workshop, and an evening meal brings him to Minato. Grandmother Chinen now lives at 5 Kitahama, a short walk from her son’s bench.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/01.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"Practical dreamer","traits":["Curious","dependable","quietly adventurous"],"habit":"Tests repaired radios at Chin and Tetsuo’s bench on quiet afternoons.","ambition":"Keep the ferry reliable and make time for home with Thuan.","favourite":"A late supper at Minato"},"article":{"headline":"The chief engineer who reads the island like a wiring diagram","standfirst":"Johansson keeps the ferry’s machinery running, with a duty cabin aboard and his primary home with Thuan on Kitano-jima.","body":["Johansson is chief engineer of the Minato Maru. His notebooks hold machinery readings, maintenance jobs and the small details that make a reliable crossing. Nakandakari is his experienced relief engineer; Tetsuo maintains the electrical equipment under contract.","He and Thuan share their primary home on Kitano-jima. He has a duty cabin aboard the ferry, while Thuan keeps her weekday room with Thao, Chin and Vy at 1 Kitahama. Neither arrangement creates another empty house in Minato.","Lunch is often at Sato Ramen. A broken radio still brings him to Chin and Tetsuo’s workshop, and an evening meal brings him to Minato. Grandmother Chinen now lives at 5 Kitahama, a short walk from her son’s bench."],"quote":"A town is a machine that runs on people remembering each other. You keep the small parts oiled.","photos":[{"pose":"Think","expression":"thinking","backdrop":"sky","film":"film","caption":"Plans for a quay that still has room for the boats.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/johansson-1.webp"},{"pose":"Laugh","expression":"laugh","backdrop":"sunset","film":"warm","caption":"Weekends on Kitano-jima.","with":"Thuan","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/johansson-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitano-1","address":"Johansson & Thuan’s House, Kitano hamlet","residents":["Johansson","Thuan"],"island":"kitano","entranceSiteId":"mayor-home"},"secondaryHomes":[{"id":"ferry-chief-cabin","address":"Johansson’s duty cabin, Minato Maru","residents":["Johansson"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"ferry-chief-cabin"}]},{"name":"Thuan","role":"Vietnamese shopkeeper","place":"Sakura Shop","bio":"From a notebook of supplier names to the island’s best-run shelves: Thuan built Sakura Shōten one delivery at a time.","backstory":"Thuan came from Vietnam after her two elder sisters, with a notebook and a plan, and wrote every supplier’s name in it the first year. Now she can tell the harbour cart from the bakery van by the sound of its wheels. The shelves are tea, rice, soap and batteries, but the regulars come for the advice, and for the onigiri she sets aside before the lunch crowd arrives. Her name is on nearly every line of the staff roster in the back room. At the bottom, in her own hand, she has appointed an assistant manager: the potted palm. In the corner by the delivery door stands a figurine of her, a present from Johansson’s shipmates. She says it is too much. She dusts it every morning before the rice comes in. She is the youngest of three sisters. Nhung, the eldest, sells books across Main Street; Thao, the middle one, runs Minato and lives with Chin and their daughter Vy at 1 Kitahama. Thuan’s primary home is with her husband Johansson on Kitano-jima, and she keeps a weekday room with Thao’s family in Minato. They trade leftovers, gossip and instructions to sleep. After closing she walks, visits Mrs Kinjō for flowers, and chooses a good meal with the seriousness of a buyer at auction. Her husband Johansson is a regular companion at those dinners. The plans for Sakura’s future are hers, and she will build them at her own pace.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/02.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"Neighbourhood spark","traits":["Warm","independent","observant"],"habit":"Sets aside a regular’s favourite tea before they ask.","ambition":"Make Sakura the shop every island household can rely on.","favourite":"An evening stroll after closing Sakura"},"article":{"headline":"Sakura’s shopkeeper knows what you need before you ask","standfirst":"From a notebook of supplier names to the island’s best-run shelves: Thuan built Sakura Shōten one delivery at a time.","body":["Thuan came from Vietnam after her two elder sisters, with a notebook and a plan, and wrote every supplier’s name in it the first year. Now she can tell the harbour cart from the bakery van by the sound of its wheels. The shelves are tea, rice, soap and batteries, but the regulars come for the advice, and for the onigiri she sets aside before the lunch crowd arrives.","Her name is on nearly every line of the staff roster in the back room. At the bottom, in her own hand, she has appointed an assistant manager: the potted palm. In the corner by the delivery door stands a figurine of her, a present from Johansson’s shipmates. She says it is too much. She dusts it every morning before the rice comes in.","She is the youngest of three sisters. Nhung, the eldest, sells books across Main Street; Thao, the middle one, runs Minato and lives with Chin and their daughter Vy at 1 Kitahama. Thuan’s primary home is with her husband Johansson on Kitano-jima, and she keeps a weekday room with Thao’s family in Minato. They trade leftovers, gossip and instructions to sleep. After closing she walks, visits Mrs Kinjō for flowers, and chooses a good meal with the seriousness of a buyer at auction. Her husband Johansson is a regular companion at those dinners. The plans for Sakura’s future are hers, and she will build them at her own pace."],"quote":"People think a shop sells things. A shop remembers people.","photos":[{"pose":"HandsOnHips","expression":"smile","backdrop":"peach","film":"warm","caption":"Behind the till: “Oldest stock at the front.”","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/thuan-1.webp"},{"pose":"Peace","expression":"happy","backdrop":"hearts","film":"vivid","caption":"After closing, with Thao, who has just woken up.","with":"Thao","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/thuan-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitano-1","address":"Johansson & Thuan’s House, Kitano hamlet","residents":["Johansson","Thuan"],"island":"kitano","entranceSiteId":"mayor-home"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Thao","role":"Izakaya owner","place":"Minato Izakaya","bio":"Thao’s izakaya opens at four. The pink telephone, the bottle keep and the karaoke all answer to her.","backstory":"At four o’clock Thao hangs the noren and the evening begins: lanterns, the pink ¥10 telephone by the door, a laserdisc karaoke that takes its time finding the disc. Regulars keep their own bottles on the shelf behind the counter, each with a tag in Thao’s careful hand. Keep one yourself and she will warn you that being a regular is worse for you than it sounds. Chin has won the last-Friday karaoke contest three times running. The police notice by the door asks drivers not to drink; Officer Mori put it up himself and then sat under it with a beer. He walked home. Thao tells this story more often than Mori would like. At three in the morning the cushions are stacked and Thao wipes the counter down while the Barfly mops. She goes home to Chin and their daughter Vy at 1 Kitahama, where her youngest sister Thuan keeps a weekday room. After sleeping until noon she buys Blue Coral cones at Mrs Nakamura’s counter, and is back at Minato by three to prep. The amber corner by the window is kept for unhurried conversations. She will not turn it into a hurried table, however full the room.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/03.webp","age":null,"friend":"Thuan","dailyLife":"Come in, come in. The spare chair has been expecting you.","islandPersonality":{"title":"The generous host","traits":["Welcoming","witty","quick-thinking"],"habit":"Keeps one spare chair for whoever arrives last.","ambition":"Turn ordinary evenings into stories people remember.","favourite":"The amber-lit corner of Minato"},"article":{"headline":"Minato never closes — it just changes who is holding the mop","standfirst":"Thao’s izakaya opens at four. The pink telephone, the bottle keep and the karaoke all answer to her.","body":["At four o’clock Thao hangs the noren and the evening begins: lanterns, the pink ¥10 telephone by the door, a laserdisc karaoke that takes its time finding the disc. Regulars keep their own bottles on the shelf behind the counter, each with a tag in Thao’s careful hand. Keep one yourself and she will warn you that being a regular is worse for you than it sounds.","Chin has won the last-Friday karaoke contest three times running. The police notice by the door asks drivers not to drink; Officer Mori put it up himself and then sat under it with a beer. He walked home. Thao tells this story more often than Mori would like.","At three in the morning the cushions are stacked and Thao wipes the counter down while the Barfly mops. She goes home to Chin and their daughter Vy at 1 Kitahama, where her youngest sister Thuan keeps a weekday room. After sleeping until noon she buys Blue Coral cones at Mrs Nakamura’s counter, and is back at Minato by three to prep. The amber corner by the window is kept for unhurried conversations. She will not turn it into a hurried table, however full the room."],"quote":"The spare chair is for whoever arrives last. Somebody always does.","photos":[{"pose":"Laugh","expression":"laugh","backdrop":"sunset","film":"warm","caption":"Last orders, which at Minato is a suggestion.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/thao-1.webp"},{"pose":"Clap","expression":"happy","backdrop":"night","film":"vivid","caption":"Karaoke night. Chin is already queueing.","with":"Chin","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/thao-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-1","address":"1 Kitahama","residents":["Thao","Chin","Vy"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"resident-home-thuan"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Mrs Sato","role":"Ramen cook","place":"Sato Ramen","bio":"Mrs Sato buys her fish at dawn, feeds the harbour at noon and washes every bowl herself. Her autograph boards tell the rest.","backstory":"Her morning starts at the harbour, choosing fish for the stock while the auction is still shouting. By half past ten she is at the pots in her own kitchen, which she shares with nobody. Lunch runs from eleven to two, and the ticket machine sells your bowl if it is in the mood; when it is not, she takes the money over the counter. Miso ramen with butter and corn is the autumn bowl, ¥500. On the wall hang three autograph boards: a television presenter who came for the Hārī, the 1996 Seagulls squad, and Kayoko Shima, who sang at the harbour hall, ate two bowls and left a tip folded into a crane. At noon Mrs Sato carries two bowls in the aluminium okamochi to the harbour office, very fast and very level. She delivers to the harbour only. Anywhere else, she says, they can walk. After two she washes up and wipes down until three, then takes the first quiet cup of the day. In the evenings she and Thao fight for the same crowd, ramen against the izakaya; they fall out, and make up over a delivery. Dr Kakazu eats here at half past twelve and is not allowed to pay. On Wednesdays the shop is shut so she can make the week’s stock. She notices who has eaten and who has only said so.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/09.webp","age":null,"friend":"Thao","dailyLife":"You look hungry. Yes, I say that to everyone. I am usually right.","islandPersonality":{"title":"Everyone’s lunch aunt","traits":["Nurturing","brisk","perceptive"],"habit":"Warms a bowl before asking how your morning went.","ambition":"Send every lunch customer back into the day well fed.","favourite":"The first quiet cup after lunch service"},"article":{"headline":"Lunch at Sato Ramen is three hours long and sixty years deep","standfirst":"Mrs Sato buys her fish at dawn, feeds the harbour at noon and washes every bowl herself. Her autograph boards tell the rest.","body":["Her morning starts at the harbour, choosing fish for the stock while the auction is still shouting. By half past ten she is at the pots in her own kitchen, which she shares with nobody. Lunch runs from eleven to two, and the ticket machine sells your bowl if it is in the mood; when it is not, she takes the money over the counter. Miso ramen with butter and corn is the autumn bowl, ¥500.","On the wall hang three autograph boards: a television presenter who came for the Hārī, the 1996 Seagulls squad, and Kayoko Shima, who sang at the harbour hall, ate two bowls and left a tip folded into a crane. At noon Mrs Sato carries two bowls in the aluminium okamochi to the harbour office, very fast and very level. She delivers to the harbour only. Anywhere else, she says, they can walk.","After two she washes up and wipes down until three, then takes the first quiet cup of the day. In the evenings she and Thao fight for the same crowd, ramen against the izakaya; they fall out, and make up over a delivery. Dr Kakazu eats here at half past twelve and is not allowed to pay. On Wednesdays the shop is shut so she can make the week’s stock. She notices who has eaten and who has only said so."],"quote":"Sit down. You can tell me how your morning went after you have eaten.","photos":[{"pose":"Bow","expression":"smile","backdrop":"cream","film":"warm","caption":"Eleven o’clock: the stools come down.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/mrs-sato-1.webp"},{"pose":"HandsOnHips","expression":"content","backdrop":"peach","film":"film","caption":"Wednesday. Stock day. Do not knock.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/mrs-sato-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-2","address":"2 Kitahama","residents":["Mrs Sato"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"resident-home-mrs-sato"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Nhung","role":"Bookseller","place":"Front-Row Books","bio":"Front-Row Books has a window seat, a cat who refuses aprons, and Nhung’s paper slips in every other volume.","backstory":"Nhung’s recommendations come on little slips of paper tucked between the pages. Pocket poetry, island histories and practical sea guides fill the shelves, and the second-hand paperbacks come with her pencil marks in the margins wherever something made her laugh. The eldest of three sisters, she still checks that Thao has eaten and that Thuan has slept. Supper is at 3 Kitahama with her husband Kōji, who comes from the fish auction, and their son Riku, who comes from school or watching the boats. She shares 1 Front-Row Yard with Reiko, so a bookshop day often continues as an argument about the evening paper. Tama the shop cat has refused, on principle and permanently, to wear the tiny apron she once made him. Johansson gets the unusual technical books first; she saves them under the counter. Her ambition is a whole shelf of books by island residents. So far it holds a tide table, a cookbook from the Community Hall and a book of poems she will not say she wrote.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/07.webp","age":null,"friend":"Reiko","dailyLife":"Window seat is mine. I bookmark the funny parts — apparently that is not how a lending library works.","islandPersonality":{"title":"A quiet enthusiast","traits":["Thoughtful","playful","attentive"],"habit":"Leaves paper bookmarks beside books she wants someone to discover.","ambition":"Build a shelf of books written by island residents.","favourite":"The bookshop window seat"},"article":{"headline":"The bookseller who marks the funny parts","standfirst":"Front-Row Books has a window seat, a cat who refuses aprons, and Nhung’s paper slips in every other volume.","body":["Nhung’s recommendations come on little slips of paper tucked between the pages. Pocket poetry, island histories and practical sea guides fill the shelves, and the second-hand paperbacks come with her pencil marks in the margins wherever something made her laugh.","The eldest of three sisters, she still checks that Thao has eaten and that Thuan has slept. Supper is at 3 Kitahama with her husband Kōji, who comes from the fish auction, and their son Riku, who comes from school or watching the boats. She shares 1 Front-Row Yard with Reiko, so a bookshop day often continues as an argument about the evening paper. Tama the shop cat has refused, on principle and permanently, to wear the tiny apron she once made him. Johansson gets the unusual technical books first; she saves them under the counter.","Her ambition is a whole shelf of books by island residents. So far it holds a tide table, a cookbook from the Community Hall and a book of poems she will not say she wrote."],"quote":"Every book in here has been read at least once on that window seat. Some of them twice.","photos":[{"pose":"Coy","expression":"shy","backdrop":"mint","film":"film","caption":"The window seat, reserved.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/nhung-1.webp"},{"pose":"Point","expression":"happy","backdrop":"cream","film":"warm","caption":"“That one. Trust me.”","with":"Reiko","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/nhung-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-3","address":"3 Kitahama","residents":["Nhung","Kōji","Riku"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-kitahama-3"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Chin","role":"Repairman and karaoke champion","place":"Dock Electrical & Repair Workshop","bio":"The dock workshop fixes everything from winches to rice cookers. It also wins karaoke contests.","backstory":"Chin lines up his tools before he touches anything new, and then he improves it. Ask him to mend a radio and it may come back with a better aerial, a louder speaker and a screw left over, which Tetsuo will find and hold up in silence. He and Tetsuo have shared the workshop since school, and their argument about volume has no closing time. Johansson stops by on quiet afternoons; the harbour office’s equipment is in their hands more often than the harbour master admits. On the last Friday of every month he becomes another person entirely: Minato’s karaoke champion, three titles in a row. The prize is a bottle on Thao’s keep shelf with his name on it. He has not opened any of them. Thao, his wife of twelve years, keeps them dusted, and their daughter Vy keeps count. On Sunday mornings he fishes off the seawall at dawn with Mr Shimabukuro. They agree on the bait and on nothing else, least of all who caught the bigger one.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/08.webp","age":null,"friend":"Tetsuo","dailyLife":"I fixed the kettle. It now whistles in a slightly more confident key.","islandPersonality":{"title":"The careful maker","traits":["Inventive","patient","dry-witted"],"habit":"Lines up his tools before touching a new repair.","ambition":"Make the docks’ workshop useful to every household.","favourite":"A finished repair that looks effortless"},"article":{"headline":"Chin’s workbench: where repairs become experiments","standfirst":"The dock workshop fixes everything from winches to rice cookers. It also wins karaoke contests.","body":["Chin lines up his tools before he touches anything new, and then he improves it. Ask him to mend a radio and it may come back with a better aerial, a louder speaker and a screw left over, which Tetsuo will find and hold up in silence.","He and Tetsuo have shared the workshop since school, and their argument about volume has no closing time. Johansson stops by on quiet afternoons; the harbour office’s equipment is in their hands more often than the harbour master admits.","On the last Friday of every month he becomes another person entirely: Minato’s karaoke champion, three titles in a row. The prize is a bottle on Thao’s keep shelf with his name on it. He has not opened any of them. Thao, his wife of twelve years, keeps them dusted, and their daughter Vy keeps count.","On Sunday mornings he fishes off the seawall at dawn with Mr Shimabukuro. They agree on the bait and on nothing else, least of all who caught the bigger one."],"quote":"A good repair should look like nobody touched it.","photos":[{"pose":"Tada","expression":"happy","backdrop":"sky","film":"vivid","caption":"Fixed. Probably better.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/chin-1.webp"},{"pose":"Cheer","expression":"laugh","backdrop":"night","film":"warm","caption":"Karaoke champion, third year running.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/chin-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-1","address":"1 Kitahama","residents":["Thao","Chin","Vy"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"resident-home-thuan"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Tetsuo","role":"Radio repairer","place":"Dock Electrical & Repair Workshop","bio":"The workshop’s radio man treats static as conversation and keeps receivers singing long after their owners give up.","backstory":"Tetsuo turns a tuning knob as though the radio might answer back, and on a good day it does. Old valve sets, ship receivers, the counter radio at Minato: he handles them gently, and he will not be rushed. Chin has repaired Tetsuo’s own radio twice. Tetsuo keeps finding extra screws. Their disagreement about volume began at the workbench when they were at school together, and it has never been settled. After lunch he crosses to the Minato Maru, where he is the ferry’s electrician under contract, and Mr Nakandakari watches him near the switchboard like a hawk. At six he goes to Umi-no-yu to tend the boiler and the pumps, and stays to close up. He takes his time over the gauges. Grandmother Higa sends him in past the bandai; he does not complain. A clear broadcast after a difficult repair is, he says, the best sound on the island, though the boiler running sweet comes close.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/20.webp","age":null,"friend":"Kenji","dailyLife":"Turn it gently. Radios have feelings. Mostly static, but feelings.","islandPersonality":{"title":"Listener to small mysteries","traits":["Analytical","gentle","absorbed"],"habit":"Turns a tuning knob as though the radio might answer back.","ambition":"Keep old receivers singing long after their owners expect.","favourite":"A clear broadcast after a difficult repair"},"article":{"headline":"Tetsuo listens to radios the way other people listen to friends","standfirst":"The workshop’s radio man treats static as conversation and keeps receivers singing long after their owners give up.","body":["Tetsuo turns a tuning knob as though the radio might answer back, and on a good day it does. Old valve sets, ship receivers, the counter radio at Minato: he handles them gently, and he will not be rushed.","Chin has repaired Tetsuo’s own radio twice. Tetsuo keeps finding extra screws. Their disagreement about volume began at the workbench when they were at school together, and it has never been settled. After lunch he crosses to the Minato Maru, where he is the ferry’s electrician under contract, and Mr Nakandakari watches him near the switchboard like a hawk.","At six he goes to Umi-no-yu to tend the boiler and the pumps, and stays to close up. He takes his time over the gauges. Grandmother Higa sends him in past the bandai; he does not complain. A clear broadcast after a difficult repair is, he says, the best sound on the island, though the boiler running sweet comes close."],"quote":"Static is just a station that has not said its name yet.","photos":[{"pose":"Think","expression":"thinking","backdrop":"cream","film":"sepia","caption":"Tuning in to something only he can hear.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/tetsuo-1.webp"},{"pose":"Shrug","expression":"grumpy","backdrop":"mint","film":"film","caption":"“He left another screw.”","with":"Chin","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/tetsuo-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"resident-home-kenji","address":"2 Front-Row Yard","residents":["Tetsuo"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"resident-home-kenji"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Officer Mori","role":"Police officer","place":"Town streets","bio":"Officer Mori walks Main Street after dark, writes everything down and still cannot get the cat to respect him.","backstory":"Mori takes the ordinary safety of the streets seriously. His night patrol is slow and exact: Main Street, the seawall, the lanes behind the shops, a look in on an elderly neighbour on the way back to the kōban. He wants the streets to feel safe without feeling watched. His reports are three pages long and supply Reiko’s evening paper with more detail than incident. The cat outside Sakura declines to acknowledge his authority, a long-running difficulty he records conscientiously. The drink-driving notice at Minato is his. He put it up himself and then, off duty, sat under it with a beer. He walked home. He would like that to be in the paper too, as an example.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/13.webp","age":null,"friend":"Reiko","dailyLife":"No suspicious activity today. Except someone teaching the cat to ignore me.","islandPersonality":{"title":"A considerate watchman","traits":["Conscientious","composed","lightly amused"],"habit":"Checks on an elderly neighbour on the way back to the kōban.","ambition":"Make the streets feel safe without making them feel watched.","favourite":"A quiet patrol through Main Street"},"article":{"headline":"Night patrol with the island’s most thorough policeman","standfirst":"Officer Mori walks Main Street after dark, writes everything down and still cannot get the cat to respect him.","body":["Mori takes the ordinary safety of the streets seriously. His night patrol is slow and exact: Main Street, the seawall, the lanes behind the shops, a look in on an elderly neighbour on the way back to the kōban. He wants the streets to feel safe without feeling watched.","His reports are three pages long and supply Reiko’s evening paper with more detail than incident. The cat outside Sakura declines to acknowledge his authority, a long-running difficulty he records conscientiously.","The drink-driving notice at Minato is his. He put it up himself and then, off duty, sat under it with a beer. He walked home. He would like that to be in the paper too, as an example."],"quote":"Nothing happened tonight. I wrote it down so we will know.","photos":[{"pose":"Bow","expression":"neutral","backdrop":"night","film":"film","caption":"Off on patrol at ten.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/officer-mori-1.webp"},{"pose":"Wave","expression":"smile","backdrop":"sky","film":"warm","caption":"Morning, after the night shift.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/officer-mori-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"koban","address":"Officer Mori’s quarters, Minato Police Station","residents":["Officer Mori"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"koban"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Harbour master","role":"Harbour master","place":"Harbour Office","bio":"Cargo, ferries, a changing forecast and a lunch delivered by okamochi: a day at the harbour office.","backstory":"He checks the tide board before the radio forecast and his knees before either. The harbour office desk holds the manifests, the passenger lists and the timetable that the duty clerk reads aloud whenever anyone rings from Minato’s pink telephone. At noon Mrs Sato arrives with two bowls in her aluminium delivery box. She delivers to the harbour only, which he takes as a professional courtesy and the harbour staff take as a perk of the job. A working harbour teaches patience with tides and with people. When equipment misbehaves he sends for the dock workshop; when a visitor is lost he points them to the right door. The freight book is his as well: every crate on the cargo quay gets a line, and every line gets his initials. He likes watching the morning cargo come in, and he likes it more when it arrives on time.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/10.webp","age":null,"friend":"Mrs Sato","dailyLife":"I can predict rain by my knees. The radio insists on taking all the credit.","islandPersonality":{"title":"Steady as the quay","traits":["Watchful","practical","quietly kind"],"habit":"Checks the tide board before the radio forecast.","ambition":"Keep the island supplied through any weather.","favourite":"Watching the morning cargo come in"},"article":{"headline":"The harbour master trusts the tide board more than the radio","standfirst":"Cargo, ferries, a changing forecast and a lunch delivered by okamochi: a day at the harbour office.","body":["He checks the tide board before the radio forecast and his knees before either. The harbour office desk holds the manifests, the passenger lists and the timetable that the duty clerk reads aloud whenever anyone rings from Minato’s pink telephone.","At noon Mrs Sato arrives with two bowls in her aluminium delivery box. She delivers to the harbour only, which he takes as a professional courtesy and the harbour staff take as a perk of the job.","A working harbour teaches patience with tides and with people. When equipment misbehaves he sends for the dock workshop; when a visitor is lost he points them to the right door. The freight book is his as well: every crate on the cargo quay gets a line, and every line gets his initials. He likes watching the morning cargo come in, and he likes it more when it arrives on time."],"quote":"The sea is always on time. It is the boats you have to wait for.","photos":[{"pose":"Point","expression":"neutral","backdrop":"sky","film":"film","caption":"Next ferry: on the board, and on time.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/harbour-master-1.webp"},{"pose":"Idle","expression":"content","backdrop":"cream","film":"sepia","caption":"Noon. The okamochi is on its way.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/harbour-master-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"office","address":"Harbour Office · residence behind the screen","residents":["Harbour master"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"office"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Miss Higa","role":"Schoolteacher; keeps the Umi-no-yu bandai in the evenings and at weekends","place":"The island school and the Umi-no-yu bandai","portrait":"assets/images/residents/higa.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"Teacher by day, keeper of the bandai by night","traits":["Patient","observant","dry"],"habit":"Marks the class’s homework at the bandai between customers.","ambition":"Send every pupil in her class on to the mainland high school able to read, reckon and swim.","favourite":"The rock bath after dark, once the boiler is running sweet"},"bio":"Miss Higa teaches the island school until half past three. From five she takes your coins at Umi-no-yu, and she still notices who has not done their homework.","backstory":"The island school is one room and one small mixed class, and Miss Higa teaches all of it. Vy asks the most questions. Riku talks, and is moved to the front row every week. Mai keeps the class rabbit, which is the best-behaved pupil and knows it. Miss Higa is twenty-nine, marks until five, and then walks down to the bath. The bandai is a raised seat by the door of Umi-no-yu, the island’s bathhouse. From up there you can see over the counter, which is the point of a bandai. Three hundred yen, swimwear in the bath, please: it is a family bath. In the evenings and at weekends she keeps it, with the marking on her knee and the crossword under the marking. The bath is her grandmother’s, and so is the opinion that Miss Higa should be married by now. Grandmother Higa says it at breakfast and again over supper. Her elder brother, the ferry’s oiler, says nothing at all and grins, which is worse. After hours Tetsuo comes to tend the boiler and the pumps. He fixes things; she does the till and pretends not to watch. He takes longer over the pressure gauge than any gauge needs. Neither of them has said a word about it, and the whole of Nishi-machi is waiting.","article":{"headline":"By day the blackboard, by night the bandai","standfirst":"Miss Higa teaches the island school until half past three. From five she takes your coins at Umi-no-yu, and she still notices who has not done their homework.","body":["The island school is one room and one small mixed class, and Miss Higa teaches all of it. Vy asks the most questions. Riku talks, and is moved to the front row every week. Mai keeps the class rabbit, which is the best-behaved pupil and knows it. Miss Higa is twenty-nine, marks until five, and then walks down to the bath.","The bandai is a raised seat by the door of Umi-no-yu, the island’s bathhouse. From up there you can see over the counter, which is the point of a bandai. Three hundred yen, swimwear in the bath, please: it is a family bath. In the evenings and at weekends she keeps it, with the marking on her knee and the crossword under the marking.","The bath is her grandmother’s, and so is the opinion that Miss Higa should be married by now. Grandmother Higa says it at breakfast and again over supper. Her elder brother, the ferry’s oiler, says nothing at all and grins, which is worse.","After hours Tetsuo comes to tend the boiler and the pumps. He fixes things; she does the till and pretends not to watch. He takes longer over the pressure gauge than any gauge needs. Neither of them has said a word about it, and the whole of Nishi-machi is waiting."],"quote":"Wash first. Then soak. Then tell me why the homework is late.","photos":[{"pose":"Think","expression":"content","backdrop":"mint","film":"sepia","caption":"At the bandai, marking between customers.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/miss-higa-1.webp"},{"pose":"Coy","expression":"shy","backdrop":"peach","film":"warm","caption":"The boiler is fixed. She was not watching.","with":"Tetsuo","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/miss-higa-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"higa","address":"Higa house, Nishi-machi","residents":["Miss Higa","Grandmother Higa"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-higa"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Grandmother Higa","role":"Owner of Umi-no-yu and Higa Liquor","place":"Higa Liquor, Umi-no-yu and her Nishi-machi verandah","bio":"She owns the bath and the liquor shop, and she knows the ferry by its whistle. From her verandah in Nishi-machi she keeps an eye on the rest.","backstory":"Sit a moment, she says, and you will. Grandmother Higa offers a seat, an observation about Sakura, and practical advice on preparing a house for typhoon season, in that order. She keeps the counter at Higa Liquor in the morning and the bandai at Umi-no-yu in the afternoon. Every bottle on the island, at Sakura, Minato or Sato Ramen, came through her door first. Her granddaughter teaches the island school and keeps the bandai in the evenings. Grandmother Higa would like her married, and says so. She has noticed that Tetsuo, who keeps her boiler running for a bath and his supper, takes his time over the pumps. She sends him through the front way, past the bandai, on purpose. Grandmother Sakurai bathes an hour before her. They have been rivals since primary school, and neither has ever let the other win a thing: not a bazaar, not a sports day, not a single argument about the price of rice. Mr Fujita soaks his back every morning and tells her the water is never hot enough. She tells Dr Kakazu the same about his advice on her blood pressure. When the Minato Maru is late she hears it in the whistle, and her grandson, who oils the ferry’s engines, hears about it at supper.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/28.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"A living island memory","traits":["Wise","playful","resilient"],"habit":"Pauses at the same shady wall on her morning walk.","ambition":"Teach the children the stories behind the island’s old place names.","favourite":"The village shade after lunch"},"article":{"headline":"Grandmother Higa has a seat for you and an opinion about your onions","standfirst":"She owns the bath and the liquor shop, and she knows the ferry by its whistle. From her verandah in Nishi-machi she keeps an eye on the rest.","body":["Sit a moment, she says, and you will. Grandmother Higa offers a seat, an observation about Sakura, and practical advice on preparing a house for typhoon season, in that order. She keeps the counter at Higa Liquor in the morning and the bandai at Umi-no-yu in the afternoon. Every bottle on the island, at Sakura, Minato or Sato Ramen, came through her door first.","Her granddaughter teaches the island school and keeps the bandai in the evenings. Grandmother Higa would like her married, and says so. She has noticed that Tetsuo, who keeps her boiler running for a bath and his supper, takes his time over the pumps. She sends him through the front way, past the bandai, on purpose.","Grandmother Sakurai bathes an hour before her. They have been rivals since primary school, and neither has ever let the other win a thing: not a bazaar, not a sports day, not a single argument about the price of rice. Mr Fujita soaks his back every morning and tells her the water is never hot enough. She tells Dr Kakazu the same about his advice on her blood pressure.","When the Minato Maru is late she hears it in the whistle, and her grandson, who oils the ferry’s engines, hears about it at supper."],"quote":"You are the one who talks to the girl at Sakura every day. Good. Somebody should.","photos":[{"pose":"Wave","expression":"smile","backdrop":"cream","film":"sepia","caption":"Sit a moment. The verandah, Nishi-machi.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/grandmother-higa-1.webp"},{"pose":"Point","expression":"thinking","backdrop":"mint","film":"film","caption":"That is not a shallot.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/grandmother-higa-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"higa","address":"Higa house, Nishi-machi","residents":["Miss Higa","Grandmother Higa"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-higa"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Mrs Nakamura","role":"Zenzai and Blue Coral shopkeeper","place":"Nakamura frontage, Main Street","bio":"Mrs Nakamura has the beans on at six, the ice-cream counter open at three and the community kitchen’s andagi in the fryer whenever the town needs feeding.","backstory":"Kintoki beans, cooked since six. The ice comes from the plant on the quay, and the old shaving machine roars like a tractor; she trusts its work completely. There has been zenzai on this corner since 1972, and she has no plans to stop. At three she serves the Blue Coral ice cream from the same Nakamura frontage for the school crowd. Her daughter Mai arrives at half past with Vy, her best friend, and the pink guava scoop is already set aside. Mrs Nakamura is bringing Mai up on her own, and does it the way she does everything, early and without fuss. She also runs the community kitchen. When there is a festival, a funeral or a typhoon, the andagi come from her fryer, by the hundred. Mrs Kinjō comes over from the park’s flower stand for lunch and asks how the ice-cream queue is doing. Mrs Nakamura gives her a second bowl and does not move the queue.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/06.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"Keeper of sweet comforts","traits":["Gentle","precise","generous"],"habit":"Remembers who wants extra beans in their zenzai.","ambition":"Keep the shop’s summer recipe in the family.","favourite":"The shaded bench outside her shop"},"article":{"headline":"Zenzai since 1972, and the shaving machine still sounds like a tractor","standfirst":"Mrs Nakamura has the beans on at six, the ice-cream counter open at three and the community kitchen’s andagi in the fryer whenever the town needs feeding.","body":["Kintoki beans, cooked since six. The ice comes from the plant on the quay, and the old shaving machine roars like a tractor; she trusts its work completely. There has been zenzai on this corner since 1972, and she has no plans to stop.","At three she serves the Blue Coral ice cream from the same Nakamura frontage for the school crowd. Her daughter Mai arrives at half past with Vy, her best friend, and the pink guava scoop is already set aside. Mrs Nakamura is bringing Mai up on her own, and does it the way she does everything, early and without fuss.","She also runs the community kitchen. When there is a festival, a funeral or a typhoon, the andagi come from her fryer, by the hundred. Mrs Kinjō comes over from the park’s flower stand for lunch and asks how the ice-cream queue is doing. Mrs Nakamura gives her a second bowl and does not move the queue."],"quote":"Zenzai is not fast food. Sit down.","photos":[{"pose":"Bow","expression":"smile","backdrop":"polka","film":"vivid","caption":"Welcome! Zenzai?","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/mrs-nakamura-1.webp"},{"pose":"Laugh","expression":"laugh","backdrop":"peach","film":"warm","caption":"Half past three: two ice creams, one of them guava.","with":"Mai","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/mrs-nakamura-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"nakamura","address":"Above Nakamura Zenzai","residents":["Mrs Nakamura","Mai"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-nakamura"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Kōji","role":"Catch sorter","place":"Fish auction quay","bio":"Kōji is up at half past four for the auction and asleep by noon. In between he sorts the catch, loads the ice and would like you to take the tuna head. It is free.","backstory":"Before the auction he crews Mr Ōshiro’s sabani, and hears again why she will never be fibreglass. By half past four he is on the auction floor sorting the catch. What the buyers leave goes to Mrs Sato for her stock pot or to the freezer, and Kōji has it sorted before most of the street is awake. At nine he moves to the ice plant and loads the boats for the next night. At noon he goes home to sleep. On Ms Uezu’s week off he works the Minato Maru’s deck as her relief, and he says the ramp is the only thing on the island heavier than a tuna. He is married to Nhung, who sells books and says he smells of fish. He says she smells of books. Their son Riku wants to come out on the boat. He may, Kōji says, when he can swim to the buoy. He likes practical food. He would especially like you to try tuna-head soup.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/33.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"The keen-eyed sorter","traits":["Quick","observant","ambitious"],"habit":"Can spot a mixed crate before it reaches his sorting bench.","ambition":"Earn the crews’ trust as the island’s best catch sorter.","favourite":"The moment the morning catch is laid out"},"article":{"headline":"The case for tuna-head soup","standfirst":"Kōji is up at half past four for the auction and asleep by noon. In between he sorts the catch, loads the ice and would like you to take the tuna head. It is free.","body":["Before the auction he crews Mr Ōshiro’s sabani, and hears again why she will never be fibreglass. By half past four he is on the auction floor sorting the catch. What the buyers leave goes to Mrs Sato for her stock pot or to the freezer, and Kōji has it sorted before most of the street is awake.","At nine he moves to the ice plant and loads the boats for the next night. At noon he goes home to sleep. On Ms Uezu’s week off he works the Minato Maru’s deck as her relief, and he says the ramp is the only thing on the island heavier than a tuna.","He is married to Nhung, who sells books and says he smells of fish. He says she smells of books. Their son Riku wants to come out on the boat. He may, Kōji says, when he can swim to the buoy.","He likes practical food. He would especially like you to try tuna-head soup."],"quote":"You want a tuna head? Free. Great soup.","photos":[{"pose":"Peace","expression":"happy","backdrop":"sky","film":"vivid","caption":"After the auction.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/koji-1.webp"},{"pose":"Laugh","expression":"laugh","backdrop":"sunset","film":"warm","caption":"Supper at home. Still offering the tuna head.","with":"Nhung","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/koji-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-3","address":"3 Kitahama","residents":["Nhung","Kōji","Riku"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-kitahama-3"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Riku","role":"Island school pupil","place":"Island school, the quay and Harbour Park","bio":"Riku is nine, Nhung and Kōji’s son, and lives at 3 Kitahama. After school he watches the boats and plays football in Harbour Park.","backstory":"Riku lives with his father Kōji at 3 Kitahama. His mother Nhung keeps her room at 1 Front-Row Yard with Reiko, and comes to Kitahama for family supper. Vy is his cousin, and the island school is part of both their days. After school he watches the fishing boats come in. Kōji sorts the catch and sometimes crews the ferry; Riku wants to join him on the boat. He may, his father says, when he can swim to the buoy. Harbour Park has room for football before supper. By half past six he is home at 3 Kitahama, with stories about the quay for Nhung and questions about the boats for Kōji.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/riku.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"The boat watcher","traits":["Curious","quick","determined"],"habit":"Watches the boats after school.","ambition":"Swim to the buoy so he can join his father on the boat.","favourite":"Football in Harbour Park before supper"},"article":{"headline":"After school, Riku is watching the boats","standfirst":"Riku is nine, Nhung and Kōji’s son, and lives at 3 Kitahama. After school he watches the boats and plays football in Harbour Park.","body":["Riku lives with his father Kōji at 3 Kitahama. His mother Nhung keeps her room at 1 Front-Row Yard with Reiko, and comes to Kitahama for family supper. Vy is his cousin, and the island school is part of both their days.","After school he watches the fishing boats come in. Kōji sorts the catch and sometimes crews the ferry; Riku wants to join him on the boat. He may, his father says, when he can swim to the buoy.","Harbour Park has room for football before supper. By half past six he is home at 3 Kitahama, with stories about the quay for Nhung and questions about the boats for Kōji."],"quote":"Dad says I can come on the boat when I can swim to the buoy.","photos":[{"pose":"Point","expression":"neutral","backdrop":"sky","film":"film","caption":"Watching the boats after school.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/riku-1.webp"},{"pose":"HandsOnHips","expression":"happy","backdrop":"cream","film":"warm","caption":"Asking the harbour master about the next boat.","with":"Harbour master","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/riku-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-3","address":"3 Kitahama","residents":["Nhung","Kōji","Riku"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"home-kitahama-3"},"secondaryHomes":[]},{"name":"Vy","role":"Island school pupil","place":"Island school, Nakamura’s Blue Coral counter and Harbour Park","bio":"Vy is eleven and lives with Thao and Chin at 1 Kitahama. After school she meets Mai for a Blue Coral ice cream at Nakamura, then takes her homework to Harbour Park.","backstory":"Vy is eleven, Thao and Chin’s daughter, and lives at 1 Kitahama. Her aunt Thuan keeps a weekday room there, and her cousin Riku lives at 3 Kitahama. The island school fills her morning; after class she has plans of her own. Her first stop after school is the Blue Coral counter at Nakamura, where Mrs Nakamura keeps the pink guava scoop aside for her. Mai arrives at the same counter. The lady says this is good customer service. Vy says it is a contract. Then she opens her satchel at a Harbour Park bench, spreads out her homework and does the hard half first, or says she will. When she is thinking she counts the pleats in her skirt. When she is not, she is watching the jan-ken-pon games in Harbour Park and keeping score for the three sisters, who all claim to be winning. She would like to be a ferry captain, or a translator, or both on alternate days.","portrait":"assets/images/residents/vy.webp","islandPersonality":{"title":"The rule maker","traits":["Bright","stubborn about small things","cheerful"],"habit":"Counts the pleats in her skirt when she is thinking.","ambition":"Pass the kanji test without the last ten characters.","favourite":"The pink guava Blue Coral ice cream"},"article":{"headline":"Ice cream before homework: the rule Vy made and keeps","standfirst":"Vy is eleven and lives with Thao and Chin at 1 Kitahama. After school she meets Mai for a Blue Coral ice cream at Nakamura, then takes her homework to Harbour Park.","body":["Vy is eleven, Thao and Chin’s daughter, and lives at 1 Kitahama. Her aunt Thuan keeps a weekday room there, and her cousin Riku lives at 3 Kitahama. The island school fills her morning; after class she has plans of her own.","Her first stop after school is the Blue Coral counter at Nakamura, where Mrs Nakamura keeps the pink guava scoop aside for her. Mai arrives at the same counter. The lady says this is good customer service. Vy says it is a contract. Then she opens her satchel at a Harbour Park bench, spreads out her homework and does the hard half first, or says she will.","When she is thinking she counts the pleats in her skirt. When she is not, she is watching the jan-ken-pon games in Harbour Park and keeping score for the three sisters, who all claim to be winning. She would like to be a ferry captain, or a translator, or both on alternate days."],"quote":"Homework is easier with guava. That is not an opinion, it is research.","photos":[{"pose":"Peace","expression":"happy","backdrop":"mint","film":"vivid","caption":"After school, guava in hand.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/vy-1.webp"},{"pose":"Think","expression":"thinking","backdrop":"cream","film":"film","caption":"The hard half first. Probably.","file":"assets/images/residents/shoot/vy-2.webp"}]},"home":{"id":"kitahama-1","address":"1 Kitahama","residents":["Thao","Chin","Vy"],"island":"minato","entranceSiteId":"resident-home-thuan"},"secondaryHomes":[]}];
